\subtree{
  \title{Decision}
  \p{\code{Vert P} sees vertical morphisms only: \code{Vert(Cat)} is the 1-category of categories and functors, and \code{Vert(Mod(Mat Kan))} has as mapping spaces the ∞-groupoids of functors and natural \em{equivalences}. Natural transformations are nullary cells into units and live in the equipment, not in \code{Vert}. Consequently:}
  \ul{
    \li{(∞,1)-universal properties in \code{Vert(Cat_∞)} are used for products, pullbacks, exponentials (functor objects), and cotensors with \code{[1]}. They suffice for these because the core of \code{Fun(E, −)} for all \code{E} (including \code{E × [1]}) detects the full ∞-category \code{Fun(E, −)}; a functor object is "an exponential in \code{Vert(Cat_∞)}", a localization "a functor with the universal property in \code{Vert(Cat_∞)}", and the concrete \code{[C, D]}, \code{C[W⁻¹]} are witnesses provided by constructions.}
    \li{Anything lax or 2-categorical — comma objects as 2-limits, Kleisli objects, lax monoidal structure, mates — is stated in the equipment \code{Mod(Mat Kan)} using cells (→ \ref{ft-0000}), never in \code{Vert}.}
  }
  \p{1-categorical universal properties in a strict \code{Cat} are never used.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Rationale}
  \p{The earlier draft called \code{Vert} "2-dimensional"; it is not. The 2-dimensional data is in the cells, and the (∞,1)-part is enough exactly for the constructions listed.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Acceptance}
  \p{\ref{at-up-0008}: for discrete \code{C, D}, the exponential in \code{Vert(Cat)} is the functor category and its universal property is the classical one on cores.}
}
\subtree{
  \title{Status}
  \p{frozen.}
}
